---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { codeThemes } from '../../code-themes';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Example from '../../components/Example.astro';
---

<Docs
  title="Forms"
  description="How Elo UI form controls handle labels, validation, native forms, and framework bindings."
>
  <h1>Forms</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every Elo UI form control is form-associated: it submits with native <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>
    elements, resets, respects disabled fieldsets, and uses the browser's constraint validation.
  </p>

  <h2>Labels, hints, and errors</h2>
  <p>
    Pass <code>label</code>, <code>hint</code>, and <code>error</code> as attributes. They render in
    the same shadow root as the control, so screen readers always announce them. Use the
    <code>label</code> and <code>hint</code> slots for rich content, and <code>hide-label</code> to
    hide a label visually while keeping it accessible.
  </p>
  <Example
    stack
    code={`<elo-input label="Email" hint="We'll send a confirmation." type="email"></elo-input>`}
  />

  <h2>Validation</h2>
  <p>
    Use native attributes: <code>required</code>, <code>minlength</code>, <code>pattern</code>,
    <code>type="email"</code>, and so on. Errors appear only after the user leaves a field or tries
    to submit, never while they're first typing. Messages come from the browser, in the user's
    language.
  </p>
  <Example
    stack
    code={`<form onsubmit="event.preventDefault()">
  <elo-input label="Username" required minlength="3"></elo-input>
  <elo-checkbox label="I accept the terms" required></elo-checkbox>
  <elo-button type="submit" variant="primary">Create account</elo-button>
</form>`}
  />
  <p>
    For server-side errors, set the <code>error</code> attribute: it shows the message and marks the
    field invalid without blocking submission. To block submission with your own rule, call
    <code>setCustomValidity(message)</code>, as on native inputs.
  </p>

  <h2>Events</h2>
  <p>
    Controls fire native <code>input</code> and <code>change</code> events, so the value is already
    updated when your listener runs.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`document.querySelector('elo-input').addEventListener('input', (event) => {
  console.log(event.target.value);
});`}
    lang="js"
    themes={codeThemes}
  />

  <h2>Frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    Each framework's own form bindings work, including checkboxes and switches binding{' '}
    <code>checked</code>.
  </p>
  <h3>React</h3>
  <Code
    code={`<EloInput label="Name" value={name} onInput={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} />
<EloCheckbox label="Subscribe" checked={subscribe} onChange={(e) => setSubscribe(e.target.checked)} />`}
    lang="tsx"
    themes={codeThemes}
  />
  <h3>Vue</h3>
  <p>
    Add <code>eloCompilerOptions</code> from <code>@elo-ui/vue</code> to your Vue plugin config,
    then use <code>v-model</code>.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`<elo-input label="Name" v-model="name" />
<elo-checkbox label="Subscribe" v-model="subscribe" />
<elo-checkbox v-for="tag in tags" :key="tag" :label="tag" :value="tag" v-model="selected" />`}
    lang="vue"
    themes={codeThemes}
  />
  <h3>Angular</h3>
  <p>
    Import the directives from <code>@elo-ui/angular</code>; <code>ngModel</code>,{' '}
    <code>formControl</code>, and <code>formControlName</code> all work.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`<elo-input label="Email" formControlName="email" />
<elo-switch label="Notifications" [(ngModel)]="notify" />`}
    lang="html"
    themes={codeThemes}
  />
</Docs>
